## Context

Follow-up to closed 1406 (PR #2216). Observed 2026-11-07 in JETP-observer, PR #246
(ticket 2102): both gaze rounds recorded ESCALATE "cleanup blocked" although the
content review had no blocking finding, so verify-gate cannot run there.
The project's post-checkout hook (`.githooks/post-checkout`) seeds every worktree
with a reflinked copy of `data/jetp/documents/` (278 files, DVC-managed, ignored),
`.dvc/config.local`, `.env`, a `.dvc/cache` symlink and
`data/jetp/releases/vnm-migration-0764.json`.

`scripts/worktree_setup_copy.py` accepts a listed path only file by file: for a
directory it requires an allowed entry *under* it (`item.startswith(name + "/")`),
so a `.worktreeinclude` naming `data/jetp/documents` (or `data/jetp/documents/`)
never makes that directory disposable. Reproduced in a scratch repo:
include `docs` → exit 1; include `docs/a` + `docs/b` → exit 0. Listing 278 files
by hand (globs are refused) is not a workable answer, and Claude Code itself reads
`.worktreeinclude` to copy files into worktrees it creates.

Impact: in any project that seeds a data directory into worktrees, every gaze
that sets up its tree ends ESCALATE; merges stall on a cleanup artifact.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] A directory listed in the primary's `.worktreeinclude` is disposable when every
      file under it is byte-identical to the primary's counterpart (symlinks under it
      still need to resolve to the primary path); one differing or extra file keeps it residue
- [ ] Red test replays the scratch case above (listed directory, identical copies → disposable;
      one hand-edited file → residue)
- [ ] JETP-observer is told by a log line on its ticket once fixed, so it adds its
      `.worktreeinclude` (project-side; not edited from the harness)
